From: Tomonori Nakagawa <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: sprd: rg-rotate: do not claim the last DRAM page

The board DT declares 0xc0000000 of DRAM at 0x80000000. Stock declares
0xbffff000 - one page less - and that page is not spare: handing it to the
page allocator produces intermittent, page-sized data corruption.

Copying a 256 MiB file from tmpfs to the SD card comes back wrong in roughly
one run out of ten, always in whole 4 KiB units and at a different file offset
each time. Everything else checks out: raw reads of the same sectors are
stable, three different cards behave identically, tmpfs is stable across
repeated hashing, and the corruption survives O_DIRECT, max_sectors_kb=32,
read_ahead_kb=4, swapoff -a, and every level of CPU and network load. What is
left is a page that Linux believes it owns and something else writes.

Match stock and stop one page short.

Signed-off-by: Tomonori Nakagawa <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
---
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sprd/ums512-rg-rotate.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sprd/ums512-rg-rotate.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sprd/ums512-rg-rotate.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sprd/ums512-rg-rotate.dts
@@ -27,7 +27,20 @@
 
 	memory@80000000 {
 		device_type = "memory";
-		reg = <0x0 0x80000000 0x0 0xC0000000>;
+		/*
+		 * 3 GiB minus the final page. Stock declares 0xbffff000, not
+		 * 0xc0000000, and leaving that last 4 KiB in the allocator is
+		 * not free: it comes back as intermittent, page-sized data
+		 * corruption once the page reaches userspace. Copying a 256 MiB
+		 * file to the SD card lands wrong data in roughly one run in
+		 * ten, in 4 KiB units, at a different file offset every time -
+		 * while the media itself is clean (raw reads of the same sectors
+		 * are stable, three different cards behave identically), tmpfs
+		 * is clean, and the corruption survives O_DIRECT,
+		 * max_sectors_kb=32, read_ahead_kb=4, swapoff and every level of
+		 * system load.
+		 */
+		reg = <0x0 0x80000000 0x0 0xbffff000>;
 	};
 
 	chosen {
